;; Zero-warning floor: every linter at :error, no :warning tier.
;; A rule worth having is worth blocking on; a finding is never "advisory,
;; fix later". Gate invocations pass --fail-level warning so clj-kondo's
;; exit-2 (warnings) is treated exactly like exit-3 (errors).
;;
;; SCOPE is a positive allowlist, not an ignore list: the gate passes explicit
;; source dirs (see LINT_CLJ_PATHS in lint.mk) and never walks the tree, so
;; generated output/, vendored renderer/lvgl/, and build targets are excluded
;; by never being handed to the linter at all. There is no exclusion config to
;; drift.
{:linters
 {;; Type safety — the rules that catch real bugs
  :unresolved-symbol {:level :error}
  :unresolved-namespace {:level :error}
  :type-mismatch {:level :error}
  :invalid-arity {:level :error}
  :missing-map-value {:level :error}

  ;; Dead code — the family most likely to catch drift in a codebase whose
  ;; job is generating fixtures (a stale import, a helper nobody calls).
  :unused-binding {:level :error}
  :unused-namespace {:level :error}
  :unused-referred-var {:level :error}
  :unused-private-var {:level :error}
  :unused-import {:level :error}

  ;; Hygiene
  :redundant-do {:level :error}
  :redundant-let {:level :error}
  :redundant-fn-wrapper {:level :error}
  :missing-else-branch {:level :error}
  :single-key-in {:level :error}
  :shadowed-var {:level :error}
  :misplaced-docstring {:level :error}
  :duplicate-require {:level :error}}

 ;; ── WHAT THIS MAP IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT ───────────────────────────────────
 ;; It is a list of LEVEL OVERRIDES, never the armed set. clj-kondo ships its
 ;; own defaults and the gate passes --fail-level warning, so a linter that is
 ;; on-by-default at :warning BLOCKS without appearing here. Counting the
 ;; entries above and calling the answer "the linters this repo arms" undercounts
 ;; it — that reading has already been made from outside and produced a report
 ;; of missing coverage that does not exist.
 ;;
 ;; Three linters an adopter reaches for are absent, each for a different and
 ;; recorded reason (`.claude/rules/gate-enforcement.md` §1: declining on the
 ;; record is honest, quietly weakening is not).
 ;;
 ;;   :refer-all — ABSENT BECAUSE IT IS ALREADY ARMED. clj-kondo defaults it to
 ;;     :warning, and lint-clj's --fail-level warning makes exit-2 fail exactly
 ;;     like exit-3. Measured: a planted `[clojure.string :refer :all]` fails the
 ;;     gate, and turning :refer-all — and only it — to :off makes that same
 ;;     input pass, which attributes the red to this clause rather than a
 ;;     neighbour. The gated tree carries zero `:refer :all` today. An entry here
 ;;     would restate a default and imply the map is exhaustive.
 ;;
 ;;   :unsorted-required-namespaces — OFF by default and REDUNDANT. .cljfmt.edn
 ;;     sets :sort-ns-references? true and `fmt-clj` is a CHECK, so an unsorted
 ;;     :require already fails a lane. Measured on one file, both tools: kondo
 ;;     armed says "Unsorted namespace: clojure.edn" (exit 3), cljfmt says
 ;;     "incorrect formatting" and prints the sorted form (exit 1). Two lanes
 ;;     refusing one input with two messages is worse than one, and cljfmt's is
 ;;     the half with a fix target behind it.
 ;;
 ;;   :consistent-alias — DECLINED. It is a DECLARED REGISTRY, not a detector:
 ;;     with {:level :error} and no :aliases map it reports nothing at all
 ;;     (measured), so it enforces exactly the namespaces someone enrols and is
 ;;     silent about every other one. Measured over the paths lint.mk gates: 940
 ;;     aliased require sites across 142 target namespaces, of which 16 carry
 ;;     more than one alias. A registry naming those 16 leaves 126 unpoliced and
 ;;     never sees the 143rd, so it cannot close the class it would be adopted
 ;;     for. Re-derive rather than trusting these numbers:
 ;;
 ;;       clj-kondo --cache false --lint <LINT_CLJ_PATHS> <LINT_CLJ_FILES> --config \
 ;;         '{:output {:analysis {:namespace-usages true} :format :edn}}'
 ;;
 ;;     Two further findings, and the second is decisive. The splits track
 ;;     TOOL-TREE boundaries — four independent projects share this one config,
 ;;     so clojure.pprint is `pp` in devcards/renderer-gen/scratchcard and
 ;;     `pprint` in protodoc, and devcards.lanes is `dlanes` only inside
 ;;     scratchcard, whose OWN namespace is scratchcard.lanes. And enrolling
 ;;     devcards.lanes at its majority alias demands something UNSATISFIABLE:
 ;;     scratchcard.lanes-test requires both namespaces, so `lanes` for the
 ;;     devcards one is a duplicate alias — clj-kondo refuses it with
 ;;     "Conflicting alias" and Clojure throws "Alias lanes already exists".
 ;;     Obeying the rule there would break the linter and the runtime.
 ;;
 ;;     The tie-break also inverts under measurement. Counting CALL sites rather
 ;;     than require sites, the minority alias is often the majority:
 ;;     protodoc.binary-dedup is `bd` at 82 calls against `binary-dedup` at 1.
 ;;     So "make it consistent" is not one mechanical rewrite; it is sixteen
 ;;     judgements, several of which argue for the shorter cryptic name.
 ;;
 ;;     This is where `.claude/rules/lint-gates.md` already puts the question —
 ;;     alias conventions sit among the splint findings that "need judgement",
 ;;     which is why splint is a tool you run and not a gate that blocks.

 ;; :lint-as maps a macro to the nearest clojure.core form clj-kondo already
 ;; analyses — never to suppress a finding.
 ;;   malli.core/=>          schema annotation, not a call — treat as a no-op.
 ;;                          (With malli on the lint classpath, clj-kondo also
 ;;                          auto-imports malli's exported type config, turning
 ;;                          m/=> schemas into real :type-mismatch/:invalid-arity
 ;;                          checking for free.)
 ;;   pronto.core/defmapper  proto<->EDN mapper macro; establishes a var like def.
 ;;   defspec                generative tests are shaped like deftest.
 :lint-as {malli.core/=> clojure.core/comment
           pronto.core/defmapper clojure.core/def
           clojure.test.check.clojure-test/defspec clojure.test/deftest}}
